  "description": "Split representations into H heads, compute scaled dot-product attention per head, and concatenate outputs.\n\nSplitting into multiple heads allows models to attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\operatorname{MHA}(Q, K, V) = \\operatorname{Concat}(\\text{head}_1, \\dots, \\text{head}_H), \\quad \\text{head}_i = \\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{Q_i K_i^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V_i\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Project and partition features into H attention heads of width d_k\n2. Compute scaled dot-product scores Q K^T / sqrt(d_k)\n3. Apply softmax normalization along key dimension\n4. Weight values V and concatenate head outputs back to model dimension D\n\n### Returns\nOutput tensor of shape (B, S, D).",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Multi-Head Attention (MHA)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Implement Multi-Head Attention (MHA)** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nSplitting into multiple heads allows models to attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions. In this problem, the goal is to: split representations into h heads, compute scaled dot-product attention per head, and concatenate outputs.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{MHA}(Q, K, V) = \\operatorname{Concat}(\\text{head}_1, \\dots, \\text{head}_H), \\quad \\text{head}_i = \\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{Q_i K_i^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V_i\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Project and partition features into H attention heads of width d_k\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute scaled dot-product scores Q K^T / sqrt(d_k)\n3. **Phase 3**: Apply softmax normalization along key dimension\n4. **Phase 4**: Weight values V and concatenate head outputs back to model dimension D\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
